<H2>Summary</H2>


<H2>The music</H2>

This album is a collection of live performances by the experimental avant jazzrock ensemble Kada. Although all tracks were recorded live, this in general is not very audible: there are no sounds of audience presence on most tracks, nor are any errors audible in the material presented. Most of the material on this album is not featured on the band's studio albums, but are improvisations of some sort, now being released.
The material shows atmospherical music working within a jazzrock vein. The throbbing bass resembles that of Mick Karn or Brand X's Percy Jones. The ghost of Brand X does float by at more of the experimental jazz moments, despite the use of sax. A lot of the time, though, the material is tranquil. And then finally, every once in a while a hint of KC guitar work of the early eighties floats by.

<P>What I like about Kada is that most of the time they manage to steer away from the endless technical diddling that would probably be great fun to musicians but tends to bore a lot of listeners (me included), except for your basic aficionado. Notable exception to that rule on this album is Isten Ordog es a Superman, which may have some roaring guitars and throbbing bass, but also a lot of whining brass.

<P>Apart from the seven audio tracks we are presented with two video (MPEG1) tracks: Tokaj and Amibientry, both in versions different from those featured in the audio section. This makes for a nice bonus. Incidentally: there was some noise on this bonus material.

<H2>Conclusion</H2>

Normally a live album can be seen as a way for the fans to relive the spirit of live concerts (did I just write that blurp of marketing lingo?). This live album, however, does nothing of the kind: it brings us mostly unreleased material that is of the same high quality as the regular studio material released by Kada. The band paints us pictures, some more lively than others, all worthwhile.<P>
